\b The Silk Road \b0 was an ancient network of trade routes that connected the East and West for over fifteen centuries. It stretched approximately six thousand four hundred kilometers, linking China to the Mediterranean Sea.\par

\par
\f1\fs20 Merchants transported silk, spices, tea, and porcelain from Asia to Europe, while bringing back glassware, wool, gold, and silver. The exchange was not limited to goods alone; ideas, religions, and technologies also traveled along these routes.\par

\par
\i Buddhism spread from India into China, papermaking techniques moved westward, and the bubonic plague devastated populations across the continent. The Silk Road ultimately transformed the course of human history through the simple act of connecting distant cultures.\par
